<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-switch-statements" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Switch Statements</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-switch-statements-structure">
        <title>Structure</title>
        <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-switch-statements">
            <title>Switch Statement Syntax</title>
            <p>A <term>switch statement</term> selects one branch to execute based on the value of a single expression.</p>
            <program language="matlab">
switch expression
    case value1
        % statements when expression == value1
    case value2
        % statements when expression == value2
    otherwise
        % statements when no case matches
end
            </program>
            <p>Key rules:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Every <pf>switch</pf> must be closed with <pf>end</pf>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The <pf>expression</pf> may be a number or a string.</p></li>
                <li><p>Only the first matching <pf>case</pf> executes — no fall-through.</p></li>
                <li><p>The <pf>otherwise</pf> clause is optional and handles all unmatched values.</p></li>
                <li><p>A <pf>case</pf> can match multiple values using a cell array: <pf>case {v1, v2}</pf>.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise label="chk-switch-end">
            <statement><p>Every <pf>switch</pf> block in MATLAB must be closed with which keyword?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>end</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. All MATLAB control structures close with <pf>end</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>endswitch</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB uses <pf>end</pf>, not <pf>endswitch</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>break</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>break</pf> exits loops, not switch blocks.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>return</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>return</pf> exits a function, not a switch block.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="chk-switch-otherwise">
            <statement correct="no"><p>The <pf>otherwise</pf> clause in a switch statement is required.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. <pf>otherwise</pf> is optional. If omitted and no case matches, nothing executes.</p></feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="chk-switch-fallthrough">
            <statement correct="no"><p>In MATLAB, if one case matches, execution continues into the next case automatically.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. MATLAB switch statements do not fall through. Only the first matching case executes.</p></feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="chk-switch-multi">
            <statement><p>To match a switch expression against either 2 or 4, you would write:</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>case {2, 4}</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. A cell array groups multiple match values in one case.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case 2 || 4</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Logical OR is not used inside a case statement.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case 2 or 4</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>or</pf> is not valid syntax inside a case clause.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case 2, 4</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>The values must be wrapped in a cell array <pf>{}</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-switch-statements-examples">
        <title>Examples</title>

        <example xml:id="ex-switch-day">
            <title>Day Name from Number</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Display the name of the day corresponding to a day number.</p>
                <program language="matlab">
day = 3;
switch day
    case 1
        disp('Monday')
    case 2
        disp('Tuesday')
    case 3
        disp('Wednesday')
    case 4
        disp('Thursday')
    case 5
        disp('Friday')
    otherwise
        disp('Weekend')
end
                </program>
                <p>Output: <c>Wednesday</c></p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-switch-string">
            <title>Switch on a String</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Switch statements work on strings as well as numbers.</p>
                <program language="matlab">
color = 'red';
switch color
    case 'red'
        disp('Stop')
    case 'yellow'
        disp('Caution')
    case 'green'
        disp('Go')
    otherwise
        disp('Unknown color')
end
                </program>
                <p>Output: <c>Stop</c></p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-switch-cell">
            <title>Grouping Cases with a Cell Array</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Classify a month number as belonging to a season.</p>
                <program language="matlab">
month = 4;
switch month
    case {12, 1, 2}
        disp('Winter')
    case {3, 4, 5}
        disp('Spring')
    case {6, 7, 8}
        disp('Summer')
    case {9, 10, 11}
        disp('Fall')
end
                </program>
                <p>Output: <c>Spring</c></p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise label="ex-switch-practice-1">
            <statement><p>What does the following code print when <pf>x = 7</pf>?</p>
            <program language="matlab">
x = 7;
switch x
    case {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}
        disp('odd')
    case {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
        disp('even')
    otherwise
        disp('out of range')
end
            </program></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>odd</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. 7 is in the first case list.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>even</c></p></statement><feedback><p>7 is not in the even list.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>out of range</c></p></statement><feedback><p>7 matches the first case, so otherwise does not run.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Nothing is printed.</p></statement><feedback><p>The first case matches, so it executes.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-switch-statements-practice-activities">
        <title>Practice Activities</title>

        <exercise label="pa-switch-season">
            <title>Season Classifier</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a switch statement that prints the season for <pf>month = 7</pf> (1=Jan, ..., 12=Dec). Group months into seasons using cell arrays. Confirm it prints <c>Summer</c>.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
month = 7;
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
month = 7;
switch month
    case {12, 1, 2}
        disp('Winter')
    case {3, 4, 5}
        disp('Spring')
    case {6, 7, 8}
        disp('Summer')
    case {9, 10, 11}
        disp('Fall')
    otherwise
        disp('Invalid month')
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="pa-switch-grade">
            <title>Grade to GPA Points</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a switch statement that assigns GPA points for a letter grade: A→4, B→3, C→2, D→1, F→0. Use <pf>grade = 'B'</pf>. Print the points.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
grade = 'B';
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
grade = 'B';
switch grade
    case 'A'
        points = 4;
    case 'B'
        points = 3;
    case 'C'
        points = 2;
    case 'D'
        points = 1;
    case 'F'
        points = 0;
    otherwise
        points = -1;
end
fprintf('GPA points: %d\n', points)
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="pa-switch-traffic">
            <title>Traffic Light</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a switch statement for <pf>light = 'yellow'</pf> that prints <c>Slow down</c> for yellow, <c>Stop</c> for red, <c>Go</c> for green, and <c>Unknown</c> otherwise.</p>
                <sage language="octave">
                    <input>
light = 'yellow';
% ==== Type your code here ====

                    </input>
                </sage>
            </statement>
            <solution component="example-solution">
                <program language="matlab">
light = 'yellow';
switch light
    case 'red'
        disp('Stop')
    case 'yellow'
        disp('Slow down')
    case 'green'
        disp('Go')
    otherwise
        disp('Unknown')
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <reading-questions xml:id="switch-statements-reading-questions">
        <title>Reading Questions</title>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-end">
            <statement><p>What keyword closes a switch block?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>end</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>endswitch</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>MATLAB uses <pf>end</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>break</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>break</pf> exits loops.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>close</pf></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>close</pf> is for figures.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-otherwise-optional">
            <statement correct="yes"><p>The <pf>otherwise</pf> clause in a switch statement is optional.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>True. Without it, no code runs when no case matches.</p></feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-trace-1">
            <statement><p>What does this code print?</p>
            <program language="matlab">n = 2;
switch n
    case 1
        disp('one')
    case 2
        disp('two')
    case 3
        disp('three')
end</program></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>two</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. <pf>n = 2</pf> matches the second case.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>one</c></p></statement><feedback><p><pf>n</pf> is 2, not 1.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>two</c> and <c>three</c></p></statement><feedback><p>No fall-through; only the matching case runs.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Nothing is printed.</p></statement><feedback><p>Case 2 matches.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-no-match">
            <statement correct="no"><p>If no case matches and there is no <pf>otherwise</pf>, MATLAB throws an error.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. The switch block is simply skipped with no error.</p></feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-string">
            <statement><p>Which data types can be used as the switch expression?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Numbers and strings.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB switch works with both.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Numbers only.</p></statement><feedback><p>Strings are also valid switch expressions.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Strings only.</p></statement><feedback><p>Numbers are also valid.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Logical values only.</p></statement><feedback><p>Logical values work (as 0 or 1), but numbers and strings are the primary types.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-cell-array">
            <statement><p>To match a switch expression against either <pf>'cat'</pf> or <pf>'dog'</pf>, you write:</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>case {'cat', 'dog'}</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. A cell array groups multiple values in one case.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case 'cat' || 'dog'</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Logical OR is not used in case clauses.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case 'cat', 'dog'</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Values must be wrapped in <pf>{}</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><pf>case ('cat', 'dog')</pf></p></statement><feedback><p>Parentheses do not create a match list; use <pf>{}</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-vs-if">
            <statement><p>When is a switch statement preferable to an if-elseif chain?</p></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>When selecting among many exact values of a single variable.</p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. Switch is cleaner when you have many equality checks on one variable.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>When checking ranges like <pf>x &gt; 10</pf>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Switch only checks equality, not ranges. Use if for range conditions.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>When you need nested conditions.</p></statement><feedback><p>Nested conditions are better handled with if statements.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Switch is always faster than if-elseif.</p></statement><feedback><p>Performance is not the reason to prefer switch over if.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-fall-through">
            <statement correct="no"><p>In MATLAB, execution falls through from one matching case to the next case automatically.</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>False. Only the first matching case executes; there is no fall-through.</p></feedback>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-fill">
            <statement><p>To handle all unmatched values in a switch, use the keyword <fillin answer="otherwise" mode="string" width="12"/>.</p></statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="rq-switch-trace-2">
            <statement><p>What is printed when <pf>x = 15</pf>?</p>
            <program language="matlab">x = 15;
switch x
    case {1, 3, 5}
        disp('small odd')
    case {2, 4, 6}
        disp('small even')
    otherwise
        disp('large')
end</program></statement>
            <choices>
                <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>large</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Correct. 15 does not match either case list, so otherwise runs.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>small odd</c></p></statement><feedback><p>15 is not in <pf>{1, 3, 5}</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p><c>small even</c></p></statement><feedback><p>15 is not in <pf>{2, 4, 6}</pf>.</p></feedback></choice>
                <choice><statement><p>Nothing is printed.</p></statement><feedback><p>The <pf>otherwise</pf> clause catches unmatched values.</p></feedback></choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>
    </reading-questions>

    <exercises xml:id="switch-statements-coding-exercises">
        <title>Coding Exercises</title>

        <exercise label="ce-switch-day-type">
            <title>Weekday or Weekend</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a function <pf>dayType</pf> that returns <c>'Weekday'</c> or <c>'Weekend'</c> given a day number (1=Mon … 7=Sun).</p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Input:</cell><cell><p><pf>day</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>day number 1–7</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell>Output:</cell><cell><p><pf>result</pf></p></cell><cell><p>char</p></cell><cell><p><c>'Weekday'</c> or <c>'Weekend'</c></p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
                    <stack><p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">dayType(3)
dayType(6)</program></stack>
                    <stack><p>Expected ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">ans = Weekday
ans = Weekend</program></stack>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
function result = dayType(day)
    switch day
        case {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
            result = 'Weekday';
        case {6, 7}
            result = 'Weekend';
        otherwise
            result = 'Invalid';
    end
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="ce-switch-grade-points">
            <title>Letter Grade to GPA</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a function <pf>gradeToGPA</pf> that converts a letter grade to GPA points: A→4, B→3, C→2, D→1, F→0.</p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Input:</cell><cell><p><pf>grade</pf></p></cell><cell><p>char</p></cell><cell><p>letter grade A–F</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell>Output:</cell><cell><p><pf>points</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>GPA points</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
                    <stack><p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">gradeToGPA('A')
gradeToGPA('C')
gradeToGPA('F')</program></stack>
                    <stack><p>Expected ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">ans = 4
ans = 2
ans = 0</program></stack>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
function points = gradeToGPA(grade)
    switch grade
        case 'A'
            points = 4;
        case 'B'
            points = 3;
        case 'C'
            points = 2;
        case 'D'
            points = 1;
        case 'F'
            points = 0;
        otherwise
            points = -1;
    end
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise label="ce-switch-season">
            <title>Month to Season</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Write a function <pf>monthToSeason</pf> that returns the season for a given month number (1=Jan … 12=Dec): Winter (12,1,2), Spring (3,4,5), Summer (6,7,8), Fall (9,10,11).</p>
                <tabular valign="top" top="minor">
                    <col width="11%" halign="right" /><col width="15%" halign="left" />
                    <col width="28%" halign="center" /><col width="46%" halign="left"/>
                    <row><cell>Input:</cell><cell><p><pf>month</pf></p></cell><cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell><cell><p>month number 1–12</p></cell></row>
                    <row bottom="minor"><cell>Output:</cell><cell><p><pf>season</pf></p></cell><cell><p>char</p></cell><cell><p>season name</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
                    <stack><p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">monthToSeason(1)
monthToSeason(7)
monthToSeason(10)</program></stack>
                    <stack><p>Expected ⤵︎</p><program language="matlab">ans = Winter
ans = Summer
ans = Fall</program></stack>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <program language="matlab">
function season = monthToSeason(month)
    switch month
        case {12, 1, 2}
            season = 'Winter';
        case {3, 4, 5}
            season = 'Spring';
        case {6, 7, 8}
            season = 'Summer';
        case {9, 10, 11}
            season = 'Fall';
        otherwise
            season = 'Invalid';
    end
end
                </program>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

</section>
